#part[What's Next?]

You have just finished a detailed look at the landscape of programming languages.

It's a broad and rich landscape with many, many dimensions to consider.

This book has largely eschewed programming language _theory_, because there are already many wonderful books about that. I recommend:

- Felleisen, Findler, Flatt: #link("https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262062756/semantics-engineering-with-plt-redex/")[Semantics Engineering with PLT Redex]
- Harper: #link("https://www.cambridge.org/us/academic/subjects/computer-science/programming-languages-and-applied-logic/practical-foundations-programming-languages-2nd-edition?format=HB&isbn=9781107150300")[Practical Foundations for Programming Languages]
- Pierce: #link("https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262162098/types-and-programming-languages/")[Types and Programming Languages]

for three rather different, but all foundational, perspectives on the further study of the subject.

Equally, programming languages are more popular than ever before. Working programmers not only contribute to their development but also put a great deal of effort into understanding more advanced features and distilling them for others. Their work tends to be found on blogs or at various conferences (such BOBkonf, !!Con (BangBangCon), Curry On, Papers We Love, StrangeLoop, and others #link("https://twitter.com/ShriramKMurthi/status/1600574419914854400")[listed here])---some are still active, others are not but have great archived content.

We're done! I hope you've enjoyed reading this as much as I enjoyed writing it!
